{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Data Communications", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 9, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.1", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about data communications.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Understand information about data and its structure in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.  Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob09re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Action Verbs to Talk About Data Communications in Different Tenses", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<strong>Action verbs</strong> represent actions people, things or processes can do.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "I know that data communication refers to the transfer of information between computers and users as well.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Don't forget it also needs a software that manages the flow of information. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Is it necessary to divide the information before the computer sends it?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "There are different verbs we can use to talk about data communications to describe different processes. <br/><br/>  Verbs like <strong>transfer, require, manage, connect,</strong> and <strong>send</strong> have different meanings according to the context in which we use them. However, when talking about data communications, these verbs acquire a more specific meaning to describe processes. <br/><br/>", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Countable and Uncountable Nouns to Talk About Communications", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Nouns in english can be countable and uncountable, they have the characteristic to adopt singular or plural forms. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "At the most abstract level, to make communication possible, data communication needs two or more computers, a device to turn information into electronic signals, and a transmission medium. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "When the computers are not connected, the data travels through a network (local or global).", "type": "example"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Countable nouns</span> refer to something we can count with numbers. They can have both singular or plural forms. <br />For example:<br />  A computer - Two computers.<br /><br /> <span class=\"strong\">Uncountable nouns</span> are the opposite, they refer to the ones that cannot be counted by numbers in plural form. That's why uncountable nouns are always singular.<br/><br/>  Some examples of countable nouns in communications are: <strong>computer, device, signals,</strong> or <strong>user. </strong>Those nouns have plural form and you can count them with numbers.<br/><br/>  Some examples of uncountable nouns in communications are: <strong>communication</strong> or<strong> software,</strong> are nouns that can't be counted with numbers. They describe generalities that usually are not quantifiable. <br/><br/>", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "What does ICT mean?", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "It stands for<strong> Information and Communications Technologies.</strong>.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<strong>ICT</strong> refers to any kind of communication device or application. They have different uses in different contexts such as education, health care, entertainment, etc.<br/><br/>   Because of the widespread and exponential evolution of technology, organizations worldwide are making great efforts to close the gap between countries that have access to technologies and those that don't.<br/>", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "transfer", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to move from one place to another", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I know that data communications refer to the transfer of information between computers and users as well.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob09aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "medium", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a channel or object used to carry or achieve something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Data communications needs two or more computers, a device to turn information into electronic signals, and a transmission medium.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob09aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "flow", "type": "name"}, {"content": "movement or stream", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Don't forget it also needs a software that manages the flow of information.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob09aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "wireless", "type": "name"}, {"content": "that doesn't need cables or wires", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: This communication can happen in different ways, from telephones to wireless connections.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob09aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "through", "type": "name"}, {"content": "that goes across something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(preposition)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: When the computers are not connected, the data travels through a network (local or global).", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob09aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "wide ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "big in size from side to side", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Because networks are wide, the data needs to travel with address and the place where it fits.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob09aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Read further information about Data communication.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Describe the new aspects and concepts you found there", "type": "text"}, {"content": "write a paragraph using the new terms found", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share it to your class.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I read further information about Data communication.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I describe the new aspects and concepts you found there ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write a paragraph using the new terms found", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share it to your class.", "type": "text"}]}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can talk about data communications.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about specific features related to data communications.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can explain briefly how data communications work.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"cont